    "result_text": "Green. Here's how I addressed the review.\n\n**Finding 1 (agree) \u2014 `ApplyOwner` open-coded the compose rule.** The reviewer is right that the \"append line2 after a single space when present\" shape lived in two places. The wrinkle they flagged in the Note is real: `ApplyOwner` must *not* normalize (the update pipeline never has), so I couldn't just call the old `compose`, which normalized internally. I resolved both by making `AddressNormalizer.compose` a **pure join** (shape only) and pushing the normalization decision to the callers:\n- `NormalizeOwnerAddress` (create) normalizes each line, then `compose(normalizedLine1, normalizedLine2)`.\n- `ApplyOwner` (update) calls `compose(line1, line2)` on the raw values.\n\nNow the join shape lives in exactly one place, and the create-vs-update difference (normalize-then-compose vs. compose-raw) is explicit at each call site rather than hidden in hand-rolled branches \u2014 which is the \"make the intended difference explicit\" point from the Note.\n\n**Finding 2 (agree) \u2014 the \"structured form supplied?\" predicate was copy-pasted.** Added `AddressNormalizer.hasStructuredAddress(line1)` and routed all three units through it: `NormalizeOwnerAddress`, `ApplyOwner`, and `RequireOwnerFields` (as `!hasStructuredAddress(...) && isBlank(address)`). The \"prefer structured when line1 is non-blank\" decision now has one home.\n\nI updated the `AddressNormalizer` class doc to reflect its widened role (normalize a line, the structured predicate, the compose shape) and rewrote the `compose` Javadoc to state that normalization is the caller's concern.\n\nOne residual `line2 != null && !line2.isBlank()` check remains inside `NormalizeOwnerAddress` \u2014 that's the *line2*-presence question (guarding whether to normalize-and-store line2 as a value vs. `null`), which is distinct from the line1 predicate the findings targeted and appears in only that one spot, so I left it inline rather than over-abstracting.\n\nTests: `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`. Acceptance behaviour unchanged.",
