    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe address-composition rule is now expressed in two places, and the change deliberately introduced a shared helper for exactly that rule \u2014 then didn't use it in one of the two spots.\n\n**Finding 1 \u2014 `ApplyOwner` duplicates the compose rule instead of reusing `AddressNormalizer.compose`.**\n`AddressNormalizer.compose(line1, line2)` was added specifically to own \"the normalized line1, with a single space and line2 appended when present,\" and its Javadoc even promises the composed value \"cannot drift from the per-line values stored alongside it.\" But `ApplyOwner` re-implements that same shape inline:\n```java\nowner.setAddress(line2 != null && !line2.isBlank() ? line1 + \" \" + line2 : line1);\n```\nSo the \"append line2 after a single space when present\" rule now lives in both `AddressNormalizer.compose` and `ApplyOwner`. This is the exact drift the helper was meant to prevent. `ApplyOwner` should call `AddressNormalizer.compose(line1, line2)` (or whatever the update path's intended semantics are) rather than open-coding the branch. A maintainer changing the composition format later will fix one site and miss the other.\n\n**Finding 2 \u2014 the \"structured line1 present?\" predicate is copy-pasted across three units.**\n`line1 != null && !line1.isBlank()` (and its `isBlank(...)` twin) appears in `NormalizeOwnerAddress`, `ApplyOwner`, and `RequireOwnerFields` to answer the same question (\"did the request supply the structured form?\"). Given `AddressNormalizer` is already the home for address logic, a small shared predicate there (e.g. `hasStructuredAddress(line1)` / reusing it) would keep the \"prefer structured when line1 non-blank\" decision in one place instead of three that must be kept in sync.\n\n**Note on the two pipelines.** `NormalizeOwnerAddress` normalizes both lines and composes via the helper; `ApplyOwner` composes raw values with no normalization. If that divergence is intentional (update path vs. create path), it's easy to miss precisely because the composition is hand-rolled in `ApplyOwner` \u2014 routing both through the shared helper makes the intended difference explicit (normalize-then-compose vs. compose-raw) rather than incidental to which code path you happen to read.\n\nThe model/DTO/schema/OpenAPI additions themselves are clean and land in the natural units; the concern is confined to the composition/preference logic being duplicated rather than centralized in the `AddressNormalizer` helper this change already created.",
